; callerid_gen.loom
; An on-hook caller ID transmitter (the North American format: Bell 202 FSK
; at 1200 baud, mark 1200 Hz, space 2200 Hz, 8N1 characters): takes a
; message's parameters from the host, buffers them, then sends the channel
; seizure, the mark signal, the message type, the message length, the
; parameters and the checksum as one continuous FSK burst, a few mark bits
; after it, and turns the carrier off.
;
; Written on 2026-11-07, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035). Caller ID is a layered protocol
; on top of a Bell 202 modem, and Loom has no modem: nothing in it was built
; for FSK. The program uses:
;   - the per-thread fractional tick (TICK_INT/TICK_FRAC, built for UART
;     baud rates): 1/132 of a bit, so both tones and the bit are whole
;     numbers of ticks (TICK_INT 315, TICK_FRAC 168 at 50 MHz);
;   - the deadline schedule (WAITD 1 every tick, proved by the checker) and
;     the deadline-latched pin write (SETP ... D, SEMANTICS 6.10, built for
;     clock-exact UART and WS2812 edges);
;   - the data memory (LD/ST, SEMANTICS 6.11, built for device emulation
;     such as an EEPROM's contents) as the message buffer.
; What the firmware does that no hardware does: the tone synthesis (the
; numerically controlled oscillator of bell202_mod.loom, copied unchanged),
; the seizure and mark signals, the parameter and message length fields,
; the checksum, the 8N1 framing, and the carrier on and off.
;
; Reuse. This program EXTENDS bell202_mod.loom rather than composing with
; it: a Loom thread cannot push into another thread's INQ (SEMANTICS 6.7:
; INQ is host to thread), so a framing thread could only reach the
; modulator through a mailbox in data memory and a shared flag, and the
; modulator would have to change to read it. The oscillator (the code from
; "tick:" to "count:", and the start-bit path) is bell202_mod.loom's, line
; for line; what changed is where each character comes from.
;
; Sources (tools/protomodels/callerid.py gives the clauses and what could
; not be checked): ETSI EN 300 659-1 V1.3.1 (2001-01) clause 5.2 (channel
; seizure: 300 continuous bits of alternating 0 and 1, the first a 0, the
; last a 1; mark signal: 180 +- 25 or 80 +- 25 mark bits; message length =
; the presentation-layer octets; checksum = the two's complement of the
; mod-256 sum of the octets from the message type on) and clause 5.3 (8N1,
; LSB first; "the data signal shall be continuous"; the checksum's stop bit
; followed by one to ten more mark bits; the modulation stops immediately
; after); EN 300 659-3 V1.3.1 clauses 5.2.1 (call set-up message 0x80) and
; 5.3 (parameter types). ETSI's physical layer is V.23; the North American
; format (Telcordia GR-30-CORE, not free, not read) uses Bell 202 tones with
; the same framing (secondary: the bell202.py sources). The tones are
; bell202_mod.loom's.
;
; Not here: the ring and the timing against it. EN 300 659-1 clause 6.1.1
; puts the burst 500 ms to 2 s after the end of the first ring and ends it at
; least 200 ms before the second; the host (or a ring generator) times the
; command word, as a line card's controller would. The signal is a
; logic-level square wave; the filter that makes it a sine and the line
; level (EN 300 659-1 table 1: -14.5 dBV +- 2.5 dB) belong to the front end.
;
; Pins: TX = OUT0 (any output or BIDIR pin with --pin TX=<pin>). Between
; bursts TX holds its last level and makes no edges: the carrier is off.
;
; Host interface (FIFO space, thread 0):
;   INQ, once, before anything else: the configuration word
;       bits 5:0    channel seizure length in blocks of 10 bits (30: 300 bits)
;       bits 13:8   mark signal length in blocks of 10 bits (18: 180 bits,
;                   8: 80 bits; the ETSI windows are 155..205 and 55..105)
;     Values below 30 and 18 are a test option, not a standard length: the
;     RTL test uses them because a full burst is 13 M clocks of simulation.
;   INQ, then, the message, one word a byte, as many messages as wanted:
;       bit 9 = 1   start a parameter; bits 7:0 its type (0x01 date and
;                   time, 0x02 number, 0x04 reason for no number, 0x07 name,
;                   0x08 reason for no name). The firmware writes its length.
;       bits 9:8 = 0  a byte of the current parameter's data (bits 7:0)
;       bit 8 = 1   the end: bits 7:0 the message type (0x80 for MDMF). The
;                   burst starts now.
;     Data bytes before any parameter header are sent as they are and counted
;     in the message length only (an SDMF body, type 0x04, is sent that way).
;     The buffer holds 124 bytes of parameters, headers and lengths included
;     (a full MDMF of date, a 10-digit number and a 15-letter name is 39);
;     bytes past it are dropped.
;   OUTQ, one word a burst, once its last bit has gone out: the message
;     length sent (bits 7:0), which tells the host whether bytes were
;     dropped. The host waits for it before queueing the next message.
; While the burst goes out the host need not keep up: the whole message is
; in memory, so the signal is continuous (EN 300 659-1 clause 5.3) whatever
; the host does. While the program waits for words the carrier is off, so
; the blocking POPs are free.
;
; Rate: the host sets TICK_INT 315, TICK_FRAC 168 before RUN (50 MHz). The
; schedule is proved for TICK_INT >= the .tick below.
;
; Memory: code 122 words, 0x000 to 0x079 of thread 0's quarter (.imem 512).
; The data spills: two variables at 0x17E and 0x17F and the message buffer
; at 0x180 to 0x1FF, thread 3's quarter, so thread 3 cannot run a program
; beside this one (callerid_rx.loom moved to thread 1, 0x080, can).
;
; Deadlines: four pairs, all proved, worst slack 0 at .tick 112: the tick
; that ends the last mark block and starts the message (it loads the end
; pointer, an LD of two slots) is 28 slots, 112 clocks. TICK_INT 315 at
; 50 MHz leaves 203 clocks of slack. The mark signal must be 1 to 63 blocks
; (a 0 there would count down from 65 535 blocks).
;
; Readings taken on the open spec questions (2026-11-07):
;   Framing from ETSI EN 300 659-1 and -3 V1.3.1, which specify V.23
;   tones; the same framing over Bell 202 tones, as North American caller
;   ID uses it, is secondary (Telcordia GR-30-CORE was not read).
;   Four trailing mark bits after the checksum.
;   Seizure and mark lengths are host options: the standard 300 and 180
;   bits are about 13 M clocks at the 112-clock tick, so the generator is
;   tested at 40/30 and 10/10 bits (the same counting code) and the
;   receiver at the standard lengths.
;   MDMF only; an SDMF message is reported octet by octet for the host.

        .imem   512
        .thread 0
        .pins   TX = OUT0

        .equ    BIT_T  = 132                    ; ticks a bit
        .equ    HALF   = 396                    ; phase units a half-cycle
        .equ    MARK   = 6                      ; units a tick at 1200 Hz (396 / 66)
        .equ    SPACE  = 11                     ; units a tick at 2200 Hz (396 / 36)
        .equ    TAIL   = 4                      ; mark bits after the checksum's stop bit (1..10)
        .equ    SEIZE  = 63                     ; r6 counts up to this through the seizure
        .equ    A_CFG  = 0x17E                  ; the configuration word
        .equ    A_END  = 0x17F                  ; one past the checksum
        .equ    BUF    = 0x180                  ; type, length, parameters, checksum
        .equ    LIMIT  = 0x1FE                  ; a byte is stored only below this
        .tick   112                             ; the fastest tick the schedule is proved for

; Collecting: r6 the next free byte   r7 the open parameter's length word
;   r0 r3 r4 scratch
; Sending: r0 phase to go - 1   r1 phase step   r2 ticks left in the bit
;   r3 the bits still to send, LSB next   r4 how many   r5 HALF
;   r6 the item: below SEIZE a seizure block, SEIZE the mark signal, from
;   BUF a byte   r7 mark blocks left, then the end pointer

start:  POP     r3                      ; the configuration word (carrier off)
        MOV16   r0, A_CFG
        ST      r3, r0, 0
reset:  MOV16   r6, BUF + 2             ; the parameters start after type and length
        MOV16   r7, BUF + 1             ; bytes before a parameter count nowhere
        LDI     r0, 0
        ST      r0, r7, 0               ; (the length word, rewritten at the end)
getw:   POP     r3                      ; carrier off: blocking is free
        MOV     r4, r3
        SHRI    r4, 9                   ; C = bit 8: the end; r4 bit 0 = bit 9
        LDIH    r3, 0                   ; the byte (LDIH keeps C)
        BC      endm
        MOV16   r0, LIMIT
        CMP     r6, r0
        BC      getw                    ; full: the byte is dropped
        ST      r3, r6, 0
        ADDI    r6, 1
        ANDI    r4, 1
        BNZ     param
        LD      r0, r7, 0               ; a data byte: its parameter is one longer
        ADDI    r0, 1
        ST      r0, r7, 0
        JMP     getw
param:  LDI     r0, 0                   ; a parameter type: its length follows
        ST      r0, r6, 0
        MOV     r7, r6
        ADDI    r6, 1
        JMP     getw

endm:   MOV16   r0, BUF
        ST      r3, r0, 0               ; the message type
        SUB     r4, r6, r0
        SUBI    r4, 2                   ; the message length: the parameters
        ST      r4, r0, 1
        LDI     r2, 0
sum:    LD      r4, r0, 0               ; type, length and parameters
        ADD     r2, r2, r4
        ADDI    r0, 1
        CMP     r0, r6
        BNZ     sum
        NEG     r2, r2                  ; the checksum: the sum is then 0 mod 256
        ST      r2, r6, 0
        ADDI    r6, 1
        MOV16   r0, A_CFG
        ST      r6, r0, 1               ; A_END
        LD      r7, r0, 0
        MOV     r6, r7
        ANDI    r6, 0x3F                ; seizure blocks
        NEG     r6, r6
        ADDI    r6, SEIZE               ; r6 = SEIZE - blocks
        SHRI    r7, 8
        ANDI    r7, 0x3F                ; mark blocks (1 or more)

        SETP    TX, 1                   ; carrier on: a half-cycle starts high
        OEP     TX, 1                   ; drive it (a no-op unless TX is a BIDIR pin)
        MOV16   r5, HALF
        MOV16   r0, HALF - 1            ; a whole half-cycle to go
        LDI     r2, BIT_T
        SETD    1
        JMP     nextc

; ---- the oscillator: bell202_mod.loom's, line for line ----
next:   SUBI    r4, 1
        SHRI    r3, 1                   ; C = the next bit, LSB first
        LDI     r1, MARK                ; 1 is mark (LDI keeps C)
        BC      tick
        LDI     r1, SPACE               ; 0 is space

tick:   WAITD   1
        SUB     r0, r0, r1              ; C = 0: this tick ends the half-cycle
        BC      count
        ADD     r0, r0, r5              ; the remainder carries into the next one
        JP      TX, 1, fall
        SETP    TX, 1, D                ; staged: lands on the next tick exactly
        JMP     count
fall:   SETP    TX, 0, D
count:  DJNZ    r2, tick

        LDI     r2, BIT_T               ; a bit boundary: the tone may change
        CMPI    r4, 0                   ; here, and only here, phase untouched
        BNZ     next

; ---- the next block or character, at a block boundary. Each starts its
; first bit here, as bell202_mod.loom's start bit does: r3 holds the bits
; after it, r4 their count. ----
nextc:  LDI     r4, SEIZE
        CMP     r6, r4                  ; C: r6 >= SEIZE
        BC      nseize
        ADDI    r6, 1                   ; a seizure block: 0, 1, 0, 1, ... 1
        MOV16   r3, 0x2AA >> 1          ; (its first bit, 0, is the space below)
        JMP     nine
nseize: BNZ     msg                     ; r6 > SEIZE: the message has begun
        SUBI    r7, 1                   ; a mark block
        BNZ     mblock
        LDI     r6, BUF & 0xFF          ; the last one: then the message
        LDIH    r6, BUF >> 8
        MOV16   r7, A_END
        LD      r7, r7, 0
mblock: MOV16   r3, 0x1FF               ; ten mark bits, the first below
        LDI     r4, 9
mark1:  LDI     r1, MARK
        JMP     tick
msg:    CMP     r6, r7                  ; C: r6 >= the end
        BC      after
        LD      r3, r6, 0               ; a byte from the buffer
        ADDI    r6, 1
        LDIH    r3, 0xFF                ; 8N1: the stop bit (bit 8), then mark
nine:   LDI     r4, 9                   ; eight data bits and the stop bit
startb: LDI     r1, SPACE               ; the start bit is a 0: space from this tick
        JMP     tick
after:  BNZ     done                    ; past the tail: the burst is over
        ADDI    r6, 1                   ; the tail: TAIL more mark bits
        LDI     r3, 0xFF
        LDI     r4, TAIL - 1
        JMP     mark1

done:   WAITD   1                       ; the edge staged on the last tick lands
        MOV16   r0, BUF                 ; carrier off: TX holds, no more edges
        LD      r3, r0, 1               ; the message length
        PUSH    r3                      ; blocks while OUTQ is full: carrier off
        JMP     reset
